381
00:19:11,530 --> 00:19:15,650
Today we're talking about something that many professionals struggle with, how to speak

382
00:19:15,650 --> 00:19:19,130
up in business meetings with confidence and clarity.

383
00:19:19,130 --> 00:19:22,250
I love this topic because it's so practical.

384
00:19:22,250 --> 00:19:26,610
You can study English for years, but if you don't speak in meetings, it's very

385
00:19:26,610 --> 00:19:29,130
difficult to grow professionally.

386
00:19:29,130 --> 00:19:30,610
That's absolutely true.

387
00:19:30,610 --> 00:19:35,370
I've met so many people who have excellent ideas, but they hesitate to share them.

388
00:19:35,370 --> 00:19:39,010
Yes, and often it's not because they don't know the language.

389
00:19:39,010 --> 00:19:41,530
It's more about confidence and mindset.

390
00:19:41,530 --> 00:19:42,530
Exactly.

391
00:19:42,530 --> 00:19:46,650
They're worried about making mistakes, or they feel that their English is not perfect

392
00:19:46,650 --> 00:19:47,650
enough.

393
00:19:47,650 --> 00:19:51,250
And the truth is, perfection is not required in meetings.

394
00:19:51,250 --> 00:19:53,610
Communication is what really matters.

395
00:19:53,610 --> 00:19:54,610
Right.

396
00:19:54,610 --> 00:19:57,530
People care more about your ideas than your grammar.

397
00:19:57,530 --> 00:19:59,970
So let's start with a simple question.

398
00:19:59,970 --> 00:20:02,650
What happens if you don't speak in meetings?

399
00:20:02,650 --> 00:20:08,050
Well, unfortunately, people may think you're not engaged or not interested.

400
00:20:08,050 --> 00:20:11,730
Or even worse, they might think you don't have anything to contribute.

401
00:20:11,730 --> 00:20:16,970
Yes, and that can affect your opportunities for promotions or leadership roles.

402
00:20:16,970 --> 00:20:22,650
So speaking up is not just about language, it's about visibility and professional growth.

403
00:20:22,650 --> 00:20:23,650
Exactly.

404
00:20:23,650 --> 00:20:27,850
Now let's talk about the moment when you want to speak but you hesitate.

405
00:20:27,850 --> 00:20:29,730
That moment is very common.

406
00:20:29,730 --> 00:20:33,410
We have an idea, but you're waiting for the perfect time.

407
00:20:33,410 --> 00:20:37,950
And while you're waiting, someone else says something and the conversation moves on.

408
00:20:37,950 --> 00:20:39,910
That happens all the time.

409
00:20:39,910 --> 00:20:43,810
So one simple strategy is to enter the conversation early.

410
00:20:43,810 --> 00:20:49,070
Yes, and you don't need a perfect sentence, you just need a simple opening phrase.

411
00:20:49,070 --> 00:20:52,670
For example, you can say, I'd like to add something here.

412
00:20:52,670 --> 00:20:54,650
Or can I share an idea?

413
00:20:54,650 --> 00:20:56,930
Or even I have a quick point.

414
00:20:56,930 --> 00:21:00,710
These phrases are very useful because they give you space to speak.

415
00:21:00,710 --> 00:21:04,430
And once you start speaking, it becomes easier to continue.

416
00:21:04,430 --> 00:21:06,990
Let's imagine a real meeting situation.

417
00:21:06,990 --> 00:21:09,870
Your team is discussing a new product launch.

418
00:21:09,870 --> 00:21:14,510
You've been listening for a few minutes and you have an idea about the target audience.

419
00:21:14,510 --> 00:21:18,750
Instead of waiting too long, you say, I'd like to add something about the target

420
00:21:18,750 --> 00:21:19,990
audience.

421
00:21:19,990 --> 00:21:21,550
That's a great entry point.

422
00:21:21,550 --> 00:21:23,430
It's clear and focused.

423
00:21:23,430 --> 00:21:25,310
Then you can continue with your idea.

424
00:21:25,310 --> 00:21:29,430
For example, I think we should focus more on younger customers.

425
00:21:29,430 --> 00:21:31,190
That's simple and effective.

426
00:21:31,190 --> 00:21:37,110
Now some people feel that I think sounds too basic, but actually it's very natural in business

427
00:21:37,110 --> 00:21:38,110
English.

428
00:21:38,110 --> 00:21:41,690
Yes, it's used all the time, even by native speakers.

429
00:21:41,690 --> 00:21:47,870
And if you want to sound slightly more formal, you can say, in my opinion, or from my perspective.

430
00:21:47,870 --> 00:21:50,750
Another useful technique is softening your opinion.

431
00:21:50,750 --> 00:21:55,750
Yes, especially if you're not completely sure or if the topic is sensitive.

432
00:21:55,750 --> 00:22:00,950
For example, instead of saying we must change the strategy, you can say I think we could

433
00:22:00,950 --> 00:22:03,390
consider changing the strategy.

434
00:22:03,390 --> 00:22:06,430
That sounds more collaborative and less direct.

435
00:22:06,430 --> 00:22:09,630
And collaboration is very important in meetings.

436
00:22:09,630 --> 00:22:12,990
Now let's talk about reacting to other people's ideas.

437
00:22:12,990 --> 00:22:17,630
Yes, because meetings are not just about speaking, they're about interaction.

438
00:22:17,630 --> 00:22:20,750
If someone shares an idea, you can show agreement.

439
00:22:20,750 --> 00:22:22,910
For example, I agree with that.

440
00:22:22,910 --> 00:22:25,110
Or that makes a lot of sense.

441
00:22:25,110 --> 00:22:30,890
Or if you want to build on their idea, you can say, I agree and I'd like to add something.

442
00:22:30,890 --> 00:22:35,150
That's a very strong phrase because it connects your idea to theirs.

443
00:22:35,150 --> 00:22:37,550
Now what if you don't agree?

444
00:22:37,550 --> 00:22:40,390
That's where many learners feel uncomfortable.

445
00:22:40,390 --> 00:22:42,790
But disagreement is normal in business.

446
00:22:42,790 --> 00:22:44,750
You just need to do it politely.

447
00:22:44,750 --> 00:22:45,750
Exactly.

448
00:22:45,750 --> 00:22:49,870
say, I see your point, but I have a slightly different view.

449
00:22:49,870 --> 00:22:54,990
Or that's an interesting idea, however, I think we should consider another option.

450
00:22:54,990 --> 00:22:57,310
Notice how we don't say, you're wrong.

451
00:22:57,310 --> 00:23:00,950
Yes, that would sound too direct and possibly rude.

452
00:23:00,950 --> 00:23:02,950
Let's do a realistic example.

453
00:23:02,950 --> 00:23:03,950
Okay.

454
00:23:03,950 --> 00:23:06,750
I think we should reduce the budget for this project.

455
00:23:06,750 --> 00:23:12,030
I see your point, but I'm concerned that reducing the budget might affect quality.

456
00:23:12,030 --> 00:23:13,350
That's a great response.

457
00:23:13,350 --> 00:23:15,970
You're not rejecting the idea completely.

458
00:23:15,970 --> 00:23:19,170
You're adding your concern in a professional way.

459
00:23:19,170 --> 00:23:21,850
Another important skill is asking questions.

460
00:23:21,850 --> 00:23:26,410
Yes, asking questions is one of the easiest ways to participate.

461
00:23:26,410 --> 00:23:29,010
You can say, could you clarify that?

462
00:23:29,010 --> 00:23:31,610
Or can you explain that in more detail?

463
00:23:31,610 --> 00:23:34,350
Or even, what do you mean by that?

464
00:23:34,350 --> 00:23:37,490
These questions show that you're listening and engaged.

465
00:23:37,490 --> 00:23:39,850
And sometimes they give you more time to think.

466
00:23:39,850 --> 00:23:41,850
That's a great point.

467
00:23:41,850 --> 00:23:44,730
can actually help you organize your thoughts.

468
00:23:44,730 --> 00:23:48,750
Now let's talk about something that happens in many meetings.

469
00:23:48,750 --> 00:23:49,750
Interruptions.

470
00:23:49,750 --> 00:23:52,610
Yes, especially in fast-paced environments.

471
00:23:52,610 --> 00:23:56,890
If someone interrupts you, it's important to stay calm and professional.

472
00:23:56,890 --> 00:23:59,970
You can say, sorry, let me finish my point.

473
00:23:59,970 --> 00:24:02,370
Or just one more thing, please.

474
00:24:02,370 --> 00:24:05,770
Or if you need to interrupt someone else, do it politely.

475
00:24:05,770 --> 00:24:09,850
For example, sorry to interrupt, but I'd like to add something.

476
00:24:09,850 --> 00:24:12,370
Or can I jump in here for a moment?

477
00:24:12,370 --> 00:24:16,370
These phrases help you manage the conversation without sounding rude.

478
00:24:16,370 --> 00:24:20,490
Now let's talk about confidence because that's really the key to everything.

479
00:24:20,490 --> 00:24:23,570
Yes, and confidence doesn't come overnight.

480
00:24:23,570 --> 00:24:25,730
It's something you build over time.

481
00:24:25,730 --> 00:24:29,610
One of the best ways to build confidence is preparation.

482
00:24:29,610 --> 00:24:33,370
Before a meeting, think about the topic and prepare a few points.

483
00:24:33,370 --> 00:24:36,690
You can even write down some sentences you might use.

484
00:24:36,690 --> 00:24:39,130
Another technique is practicing out loud.

485
00:24:39,130 --> 00:24:42,650
Yes, speaking silently in your head is not enough.

486
00:24:42,650 --> 00:24:47,130
When you speak out loud, you train your brain and your mouth to work together.

487
00:24:47,130 --> 00:24:50,170
That makes a big difference in real situations.

488
00:24:50,170 --> 00:24:53,450
Also, don't try to say everything perfectly.

489
00:24:53,450 --> 00:24:56,330
Focus on being clear, not perfect.

490
00:24:56,330 --> 00:24:59,130
Let's imagine a longer meeting scenario.

491
00:24:59,130 --> 00:24:59,930
Okay.

492
00:24:59,930 --> 00:25:02,650
The team is discussing a marketing campaign.

493
00:25:02,650 --> 00:25:07,130
You've been listening for a few minutes and you have an idea about the campaign.

494
00:25:07,130 --> 00:25:10,570
You say, I'd like to share an idea about the campaign.

495
00:25:10,570 --> 00:25:14,850
Then you continue, I think we should focus more on digital channels.

496
00:25:14,850 --> 00:25:18,250
Someone responds, that's interesting, why do you think that?

497
00:25:18,250 --> 00:25:24,050
You answer, because our target audience spends more time online, especially on social media.

498
00:25:24,050 --> 00:25:29,130
Another colleague says, I see your point, but traditional marketing is still important.

499
00:25:29,130 --> 00:25:34,250
You reply, I agree, maybe we can combine both approaches for better results.

500
00:25:34,250 --> 00:25:37,490
That's a perfect example of a balanced discussion.

501
00:25:37,490 --> 00:25:40,370
Yes, and notice how simple the language is.

502
00:25:40,370 --> 00:25:45,010
Exactly. You don't need complex vocabulary to communicate effectively.

503
00:25:45,010 --> 00:25:48,610
Now, let's talk about a common problem, overthinking.

504
00:25:48,610 --> 00:25:53,130
Yes, many learners try to create perfect sentences in their heads.

505
00:25:53,130 --> 00:25:56,330
And by the time they're ready, the opportunity is gone.

506
00:25:56,330 --> 00:25:59,810
So instead, think simply and speak quickly.

507
00:25:59,810 --> 00:26:02,850
Not too fast, but without too much delay.

508
00:26:02,850 --> 00:26:05,810
Another mistake is speaking too softly.

509
00:26:05,810 --> 00:26:07,610
Yes, volume matters.

510
00:26:07,610 --> 00:26:09,690
People need to hear you clearly.

511
00:26:09,690 --> 00:26:12,290
And eye contact is also important.

512
00:26:12,290 --> 00:26:14,690
It shows confidence and engagement.

513
00:26:14,690 --> 00:26:17,290
Now let's talk about small steps.

514
00:26:17,290 --> 00:26:20,690
Yes, you don't need to become very active immediately.

515
00:26:20,690 --> 00:26:23,110
Start with one sentence per meeting.

516
00:26:23,110 --> 00:26:25,930
Then gradually increase your participation.

517
00:26:25,930 --> 00:26:28,450
Over time, it will feel more natural.

518
00:26:28,450 --> 00:26:31,410
And remember, everyone started somewhere.

519
00:26:31,410 --> 00:26:34,330
Even confidence speakers were once beginners.

520
00:26:34,330 --> 00:26:35,770
That's very true.

521
00:26:35,770 --> 00:26:38,810
So don't compare yourself too much to others.

522
00:26:38,810 --> 00:26:41,030
Focus on your own progress.

523
00:26:41,030 --> 00:26:43,290
Every time you speak, you improve.

524
00:26:43,290 --> 00:26:46,310
And every small step builds your confidence.

525
00:26:46,310 --> 00:26:48,050
So next time you're in a meeting,

526
00:26:48,050 --> 00:26:50,210
try to speak at least once.

527
00:26:50,210 --> 00:26:53,570
Even if it's just a short comment or a simple question.

528
00:26:53,570 --> 00:26:55,530
Because your ideas matter.

529
00:26:55,530 --> 00:26:58,010
And your voice deserves to be heard.

