547
00:27:34,030 --> 00:27:38,110
And today, we're going to take a deeper and more comprehensive look at a topic that many

548
00:27:38,110 --> 00:27:42,750
professionals struggle with, how to speak up in business meetings with confidence.

549
00:27:42,750 --> 00:27:49,030
Yes, and this is not just about language, it's about confidence, mindset, preparation,

550
00:27:49,030 --> 00:27:50,990
and communication strategy.

551
00:27:50,990 --> 00:27:56,630
Exactly, because even people with strong English skills sometimes stay silent in meetings.

552
00:27:56,630 --> 00:27:57,710
That's very true.

553
00:27:57,710 --> 00:28:02,830
So if you've ever had a good idea but didn't say it, or if you felt nervous speaking in

554
00:28:02,830 --> 00:28:06,670
front of others, this conversation is for you.

555
00:28:06,670 --> 00:28:09,430
Let's start by understanding the problem more clearly.

556
00:28:09,430 --> 00:28:12,390
Why do people hesitate to speak in meetings?

557
00:28:12,390 --> 00:28:14,150
There are several reasons.

558
00:28:14,150 --> 00:28:16,730
One of the biggest is fear of judgment.

559
00:28:16,730 --> 00:28:19,070
People worry about how others will react.

560
00:28:19,070 --> 00:28:23,470
Yes, especially in professional environments where you may be speaking to managers

561
00:28:23,470 --> 00:28:25,550
or senior leaders.

562
00:28:25,550 --> 00:28:30,710
reason is lack of confidence in English. People think their grammar isn't perfect or their

563
00:28:30,710 --> 00:28:33,070
pronunciation isn't clear.

564
00:28:33,070 --> 00:28:37,030
And that creates hesitation. They start overthinking instead of speaking.

565
00:28:37,030 --> 00:28:42,430
Exactly. And there's also the issue of timing. Some people don't know when to enter the

566
00:28:42,430 --> 00:28:43,430
conversation.

567
00:28:43,430 --> 00:28:47,790
Right. They wait for the perfect moment, but that moment never comes.

568
00:28:47,790 --> 00:28:52,870
So, let's solve this step by step. First, mindset.

569
00:28:52,870 --> 00:28:54,230
This is the foundation.

570
00:28:54,230 --> 00:28:57,630
If your mindset is weak, your communication will be weak.

571
00:28:57,630 --> 00:29:03,550
Instead of thinking, my English is not good enough, try thinking, I can communicate clearly

572
00:29:03,550 --> 00:29:05,550
with simple English.

573
00:29:05,550 --> 00:29:06,990
That's a powerful shift.

574
00:29:06,990 --> 00:29:10,590
You don't need advanced vocabulary to contribute in a meeting.

575
00:29:10,590 --> 00:29:11,590
Exactly.

576
00:29:11,590 --> 00:29:17,550
Simple, clear, and direct communication is often more effective than complex language.

577
00:29:17,550 --> 00:29:21,350
Let's move to preparation, which is one of the most practical strategies.

578
00:29:21,350 --> 00:29:25,350
Yes. Before any meeting, take a few minutes to prepare your thoughts.

579
00:29:25,990 --> 00:29:30,550
Ask yourself, what is the purpose of this meeting? What topics will be discussed?

580
00:29:30,550 --> 00:29:32,150
Where can I contribute?

581
00:29:32,150 --> 00:29:36,390
And most importantly, prepare at least one point you want to say.

582
00:29:37,030 --> 00:29:42,070
That's key. If you go into a meeting with zero preparation, your confidence will be low.

583
00:29:42,630 --> 00:29:46,870
But if you already have one idea ready, you're much more likely to speak.

584
00:29:47,510 --> 00:29:48,950
Let's give an example.

585
00:29:48,950 --> 00:29:53,950
Sure. Imagine there's a meeting about improving customer satisfaction.

586
00:29:53,950 --> 00:29:55,950
You could prepare a simple sentence like,

587
00:29:55,950 --> 00:29:59,950
I think we should collect more customer feedback before making changes.

588
00:29:59,950 --> 00:30:02,950
That's clear, professional, and easy to say.

589
00:30:02,950 --> 00:30:05,950
Now, let's talk about how to start speaking.

590
00:30:05,950 --> 00:30:07,950
This is where many people get stuck.

591
00:30:07,950 --> 00:30:11,950
Yes. The opening sentence is often the hardest part.

592
00:30:11,950 --> 00:30:14,950
Here are some simple and powerful phrases.

593
00:30:14,950 --> 00:30:16,950
I'd like to add something.

594
00:30:16,950 --> 00:30:18,950
Can I share an idea?

595
00:30:18,950 --> 00:30:20,950
I have a quick suggestion.

596
00:30:20,950 --> 00:30:23,950
These phrases are your entry point into the conversation.

597
00:30:23,950 --> 00:30:28,950
Once you say that first sentence, your confidence naturally increases.

598
00:30:28,950 --> 00:30:31,950
Exactly. Action reduces fear.

599
00:30:31,950 --> 00:30:34,950
Now let's talk about structuring your ideas.

600
00:30:34,950 --> 00:30:39,950
Yes, because speaking is not just about talking, it's about organizing your thoughts.

601
00:30:39,950 --> 00:30:45,950
A simple structure you can follow is idea, reason, example.

602
00:30:45,950 --> 00:30:47,750
That's very effective.

603
00:30:47,750 --> 00:30:51,850
For example, I think we should change our marketing strategy

604
00:30:51,850 --> 00:30:55,050
because our current approach is not reaching enough customers.

605
00:30:55,050 --> 00:30:59,050
For example, our last campaign had low engagement.

606
00:30:59,050 --> 00:31:01,950
That sounds clear, logical, and professional.

607
00:31:01,950 --> 00:31:05,950
Now let's discuss agreeing and disagreeing politely.

608
00:31:05,950 --> 00:31:08,450
This is very important in meetings.

609
00:31:08,450 --> 00:31:13,150
To agree, you can say, I agree with that, or that's a good point.

610
00:31:13,150 --> 00:31:15,650
And then you can add your own idea.

611
00:31:15,650 --> 00:31:19,150
To disagree, you don't need to be direct or aggressive.

612
00:31:19,150 --> 00:31:23,990
Yes, you can say, I see your point, but I have a slightly different view.

613
00:31:23,990 --> 00:31:28,610
Or, that's interesting, but maybe we could consider another approach.

614
00:31:28,610 --> 00:31:31,890
That keeps the conversation professional and respectful.

615
00:31:31,890 --> 00:31:34,590
Now let's talk about asking questions.

616
00:31:34,590 --> 00:31:38,430
Asking questions is actually one of the easiest ways to participate.

617
00:31:38,430 --> 00:31:43,530
Yes, because you don't need to have a perfect idea, you just need curiosity.

618
00:31:43,530 --> 00:31:45,970
You can say, could you clarify that?

619
00:31:45,970 --> 00:31:48,490
Or, what do you mean by that?

620
00:31:48,490 --> 00:31:50,610
Or, can you give an example?

621
00:31:50,610 --> 00:31:52,530
These questions show engagement

622
00:31:52,530 --> 00:31:54,450
and help you understand better.

623
00:31:54,450 --> 00:31:57,170
Now, let's address fear of mistakes.

624
00:31:57,170 --> 00:32:00,370
This is something almost everyone experiences.

625
00:32:00,370 --> 00:32:01,490
But here's the truth.

626
00:32:01,490 --> 00:32:03,770
Mistakes are part of communication.

627
00:32:03,770 --> 00:32:07,090
Even experienced professionals make mistakes.

628
00:32:07,090 --> 00:32:10,290
What matters is your message, not perfection.

629
00:32:10,290 --> 00:32:11,290
Exactly.

630
00:32:11,290 --> 00:32:14,730
People remember ideas, not grammar errors.

631
00:32:14,730 --> 00:32:17,450
Now, let's talk about voice and delivery.

632
00:32:17,450 --> 00:32:21,130
Yes, this is often overlooked, but very important.

633
00:32:21,130 --> 00:32:23,130
Speak at a steady pace.

634
00:32:23,130 --> 00:32:25,170
Don't rush your words.

635
00:32:25,170 --> 00:32:27,650
Use pauses to organize your thoughts.

636
00:32:27,650 --> 00:32:31,010
And make sure your voice is clear and audible.

637
00:32:31,010 --> 00:32:33,970
Even if you feel nervous, try to sound calm.

638
00:32:33,970 --> 00:32:38,050
Confidence in your voice creates confidence in your message.

639
00:32:38,050 --> 00:32:40,090
Now, let's discuss timing.

640
00:32:40,090 --> 00:32:43,210
Timing is a skill you develop with practice.

641
00:32:43,210 --> 00:32:45,930
Look for natural pauses in the conversation.

642
00:32:45,930 --> 00:32:49,610
When someone finishes speaking, that's your opportunity.

643
00:32:49,610 --> 00:32:51,770
You can also use body language.

644
00:32:51,770 --> 00:32:55,450
Yes, like leaning forward or making eye contact.

645
00:32:55,450 --> 00:32:58,090
These signals show you want to contribute.

646
00:32:58,090 --> 00:33:02,250
Now let's talk about online meetings which are very common today.

647
00:33:02,250 --> 00:33:04,890
Yes, and they come with their own challenges.

648
00:33:04,890 --> 00:33:08,090
In online meetings it's easy to stay silent.

649
00:33:08,090 --> 00:33:10,130
but you can use tools to help you.

650
00:33:10,130 --> 00:33:13,890
For example, use the chat box to share ideas.

651
00:33:13,890 --> 00:33:17,690
Or use the raise hand feature to indicate you want to speak.

652
00:33:17,690 --> 00:33:21,130
Another tip is to turn on your camera if possible.

653
00:33:21,130 --> 00:33:23,590
That helps you stay engaged and visible.

654
00:33:23,590 --> 00:33:26,890
Now let's talk about handling interruptions.

655
00:33:26,890 --> 00:33:28,990
This happens often in meetings.

656
00:33:28,990 --> 00:33:31,390
When someone interrupts you, stay calm.

657
00:33:31,390 --> 00:33:32,290
You can say,

658
00:33:32,290 --> 00:33:35,090
Sorry, can I just finish my point?

659
00:33:35,090 --> 00:33:37,690
Or I'll be quick, just one more point.

660
00:33:37,690 --> 00:33:41,130
These phrases help you stay in control of your message.

661
00:33:41,130 --> 00:33:44,410
Now, let's discuss building long-term confidence.

662
00:33:44,410 --> 00:33:48,090
Yes, because this is not something that changes overnight.

663
00:33:48,090 --> 00:33:51,810
Start small. Speak in once in every meeting.

664
00:33:51,810 --> 00:33:54,810
Then gradually increase your participation.

665
00:33:54,810 --> 00:33:57,330
Practice outside meetings as well.

666
00:33:57,330 --> 00:34:01,130
Speak out loud. Record yourself or practice with a partner.

667
00:34:01,130 --> 00:34:04,290
Over time, speaking will feel more natural.

668
00:34:04,290 --> 00:34:07,210
And your confidence will grow step by step.

669
00:34:07,210 --> 00:34:10,810
Let's do a quick example of a full contribution.

670
00:34:10,810 --> 00:34:11,850
Great idea!

671
00:34:11,850 --> 00:34:13,530
I'd like to add something.

672
00:34:13,530 --> 00:34:18,090
I think we should review our current process because it's taking too much time.

673
00:34:18,090 --> 00:34:22,010
For example, our last project was delayed by two weeks.

674
00:34:22,010 --> 00:34:24,570
Maybe we can simplify the approval steps.

675
00:34:25,130 --> 00:34:26,890
That's an excellent example.

676
00:34:26,890 --> 00:34:29,290
Clear, structured, and professional.

677
00:34:29,290 --> 00:34:31,370
And notice the language is simple.

678
00:34:32,010 --> 00:34:33,770
Yes, but the impact is strong.

679
00:34:34,410 --> 00:34:36,170
Now let's summarize everything.

680
00:34:36,170 --> 00:34:40,730
First, focus on clear communication, not perfect English.

681
00:34:40,730 --> 00:34:44,730
First, prepare at least one idea before the meeting.

682
00:34:44,730 --> 00:34:48,010
Third, use simple phrases to start speaking.

683
00:34:48,010 --> 00:34:50,890
Fourth, structure your ideas clearly.

684
00:34:50,890 --> 00:34:54,090
Fifth, don't be afraid to make mistakes.

685
00:34:54,090 --> 00:34:58,170
Sixth, practice regularly and start small.

686
00:34:58,170 --> 00:35:02,130
If you follow these steps, you will become more confident in meetings.

687
00:35:02,130 --> 00:35:05,130
And remember, your voice matters.

688
00:35:05,130 --> 00:35:07,050
Wait for the perfect moment.

689
00:35:07,050 --> 00:35:09,770
Create your moment by speaking up.

